1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,090
Tanjong Katong by the Sea:

2
00:00:02,170 --> 00:00:05,925
From Dunman's Bungalows
to the Seaside Resort

3
00:00:05,925 --> 00:00:07,615
For more than a century,

4
00:00:07,695 --> 00:00:11,750
Tanjong Katong was where
Singapore went to the seaside.

5
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:16,361
Meyer Road and Marine Parade Road
ran along the beach, and many of

6
00:00:16,441 --> 00:00:21,275
the houses on their seaward side
had gardens that ended at the sand.

7
00:00:21,275 --> 00:00:24,574
The sea is now several
hundred metres away,

8
00:00:24,654 --> 00:00:29,840
beyond East Coast Park and the East
Coast Parkway, and a station on

9
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:34,400
the Thomson–East Coast Line
opened on Meyer Road in 2024.

10
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:39,026
How a coconut coast became a resort,
then a suburb of seaside houses,

11
00:00:39,106 --> 00:00:42,095
and finally an inland
neighbourhood is also a

12
00:00:42,175 --> 00:00:46,051
story about property: what
people paid to live by the sea,

13
00:00:46,131 --> 00:00:49,950
and what happened to their
land when the sea moved away.

14
00:00:49,950 --> 00:00:54,800
Tanjong Katong takes its name
from the Malay for "turtle point".

15
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:57,755
In the first decades of
British Singapore its

16
00:00:57,835 --> 00:01:00,790
sandy shore east of the
town was planted with

17
00:01:00,870 --> 00:01:04,175
cotton and then with
coconuts, which grew better.

18
00:01:04,175 --> 00:01:10,522
By the 1830s there were about 660
acres of coconut palms on the island,

19
00:01:10,602 --> 00:01:14,675
mostly at Tanjong Katong
and on Blakang Mati.

20
00:01:14,675 --> 00:01:20,182
The largest estate belonged to
Thomas Dunman, who ran Singapore's

21
00:01:20,262 --> 00:01:25,075
police force from 1843 to
1871 (his story is told here).

22
00:01:25,075 --> 00:01:28,218
His coconut plantation of about 400 acres,

23
00:01:28,298 --> 00:01:33,282
later known as Grove Estate,
stretched from the old Tanjong Katong

24
00:01:33,362 --> 00:01:37,275
Road to the sea along what
is now Mountbatten Road.

25
00:01:37,275 --> 00:01:41,550
He built his own house there,
and three bungalows on the shore.

26
00:01:41,550 --> 00:01:45,595
The historian Charles Burton
Buckley, writing in 1902,

27
00:01:45,675 --> 00:01:48,956
called them "the first
of the Tanjong Katong

28
00:01:49,036 --> 00:01:52,550
waterside houses for
honeymoons and holidays".

29
00:01:52,550 --> 00:01:56,150
Dunman's bungalows were
not alone for long.

30
00:01:56,150 --> 00:02:00,317
The 1868 street directory
listed 14 holiday houses,

31
00:02:00,397 --> 00:02:04,481
or "sanitaria", around the
island, and six of them

32
00:02:04,561 --> 00:02:08,725
were cottages at "Dunman
Ville" in Tanjong Katong.

33
00:02:08,725 --> 00:02:11,747
A Tanjong Katong Hotel opened in 1883,

34
00:02:11,827 --> 00:02:16,889
and wealthy Straits Chinese,
European and Jewish families began

35
00:02:16,969 --> 00:02:21,050
to build their own weekend
houses along the beach.

36
00:02:21,050 --> 00:02:24,025
One of those houses still stands.

37
00:02:24,025 --> 00:02:27,658
Sea Breeze Lodge, at
37 Marine Parade Road,

38
00:02:27,738 --> 00:02:31,112
was completed in 1898 for Choa-Kim-Keat,

39
00:02:31,192 --> 00:02:35,250
a businessman with the
Straits Trading Company.

40
00:02:35,250 --> 00:02:38,920
It was a single-storey bungalow
raised above the ground,

41
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,599
with a staircase up to a front verandah,

42
00:02:41,679 --> 00:02:44,625
and it stood about five
metres from the sea.

43
00:02:44,625 --> 00:02:48,675
The Choa family used it
as a weekend retreat.

44
00:02:48,675 --> 00:02:52,425
By the turn of the century
the beach had filled up.

45
00:02:52,425 --> 00:02:56,483
Buckley noted that Dunman's
three bungalows "were not added

46
00:02:56,563 --> 00:03:00,060
to for many years", but by
1902 "the whole beach is

47
00:03:00,140 --> 00:03:04,127
overrun with them, and the
land divided and sub-divided to

48
00:03:04,207 --> 00:03:08,275
such an extent and built over
that the enjoyment is gone".

49
00:03:08,275 --> 00:03:11,148
It is one of the earliest
descriptions of a

50
00:03:11,228 --> 00:03:14,800
Singapore seafront being
parcelled out for property.

51
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:19,250
The best known address on the
beach was the Sea View Hotel.

52
00:03:19,250 --> 00:03:23,920
It opened on Saturday, the 2nd
of June 1906, in a converted

53
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,348
bungalow owned by Manasseh
Meyer, a Jewish merchant and

54
00:03:28,428 --> 00:03:33,500
property owner whose name the
road in front of it took in 1921.

55
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:37,518
The Straits Times reported on
the opening day that guests

56
00:03:37,598 --> 00:03:41,625
began arriving "by sea and
road" soon after two o'clock.

57
00:03:41,625 --> 00:03:45,395
The hotel had 40 rooms and
stood near the terminus of

58
00:03:45,475 --> 00:03:49,325
the tram line, which made
it easy to reach from town.

59
00:03:49,325 --> 00:03:52,568
The Sarkies brothers,
who ran Raffles Hotel,

60
00:03:52,648 --> 00:03:56,570
took over its management in
1923 and added a new wing

61
00:03:56,650 --> 00:04:00,275
and a bathing house with
a shark-proof sea pool.

62
00:04:00,275 --> 00:04:03,816
In the 1930s it was
counted among Singapore's

63
00:04:03,896 --> 00:04:08,000
three leading hotels, with
Raffles and the Adelphi.

64
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,820
By the 1950s it had 69
rooms, and dances were

65
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:15,800
held in its ballroom
most nights of the week.

66
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:21,960
During the Japanese Occupation it was
renamed and used by the occupiers,

67
00:04:22,040 --> 00:04:26,200
and it reopened to civilian
guests in July 1946.

68
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:29,650
The beach also had its swimming clubs.

69
00:04:29,650 --> 00:04:33,671
The Chinese Swimming Club
began in 1905 as a group of

70
00:04:33,751 --> 00:04:38,314
Straits Chinese men who called
themselves the Tanjong Katong

71
00:04:38,394 --> 00:04:42,650
Swimming Party and swam off
Marine Parade every Sunday.

72
00:04:42,650 --> 00:04:46,417
It renamed itself in
1910, and around 1921 it

73
00:04:46,497 --> 00:04:49,666
moved to Amber Road, renting a seaside

74
00:04:49,746 --> 00:04:53,850
bungalow from the
philanthropist Lee Choon Guan.

75
00:04:53,850 --> 00:04:57,505
It was the Chinese answer
to the European-only

76
00:04:57,585 --> 00:05:02,050
Singapore Swimming Club,
which had been set up in 1894.

77
00:05:02,050 --> 00:05:06,210
In 1939 the club built a
three-storey clubhouse and a

78
00:05:06,290 --> 00:05:10,850
concrete pool that was filled
with seawater at high tide.

79
00:05:10,850 --> 00:05:16,043
As the town grew crowded in the early
20th century, more people moved east,

80
00:05:16,123 --> 00:05:20,200
and the old plantations were
broken up into building lots.

81
00:05:20,200 --> 00:05:25,150
Seafront land became some of
the most desirable in Singapore.

82
00:05:25,150 --> 00:05:29,464
In 1909 Manasseh Meyer built
Crescent Flats on the beach,

83
00:05:29,544 --> 00:05:34,474
a curved block thought to be among
the earliest luxury beachfront

84
00:05:34,554 --> 00:05:39,025
apartments in Singapore, and
Meyer Flats followed in 1928.

85
00:05:39,025 --> 00:05:43,900
Along Meyer Road, families built
large houses facing the sea.

86
00:05:43,900 --> 00:05:47,388
After the Second World
War, the public beach along

87
00:05:47,468 --> 00:05:51,312
Marine Parade was popular
with swimmers and picnickers,

88
00:05:51,392 --> 00:05:54,175
and hawkers sold food on the promenade.

89
00:05:54,175 --> 00:05:58,380
In 1952 the seawall had decayed
so badly that a nearby road

90
00:05:58,460 --> 00:06:03,173
was in danger of falling into the
sea, and after a dispute between

91
00:06:03,253 --> 00:06:07,893
the colonial government and the
city council over who should pay,

92
00:06:07,973 --> 00:06:11,888
the council was given $318,000
to repair it and lay out

93
00:06:11,968 --> 00:06:16,325
a seaside promenade with changing
rooms and lifeguard posts.

94
00:06:16,325 --> 00:06:20,225
The resort era ended in the 1960s.

95
00:06:20,225 --> 00:06:25,561
The Sea View Hotel closed in
early 1964, after its operating

96
00:06:25,641 --> 00:06:29,984
company went into liquidation,
and in August that

97
00:06:30,064 --> 00:06:35,300
year its 359,600-square-foot
property was put up for sale.

98
00:06:35,300 --> 00:06:38,875
The hotel was demolished soon afterwards.

99
00:06:38,875 --> 00:06:42,496
A new Sea View Hotel, an 18-storey tower,

100
00:06:42,576 --> 00:06:46,197
opened nearby in 1969, and it was in turn

101
00:06:46,277 --> 00:06:50,700
demolished in 2003 to make
way for a condominium.

102
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:55,247
In 1966 the government began
the East Coast reclamation,

103
00:06:55,327 --> 00:07:00,450
the largest land reclamation
project in Singapore at the time.

104
00:07:00,450 --> 00:07:03,824
Hills at Siglap, Bedok and
Tampines were cut down,

105
00:07:03,904 --> 00:07:07,622
and their earth was carried
by conveyor belt to a jetty

106
00:07:07,702 --> 00:07:11,225
off Bedok and taken out by
barge, around the clock.

107
00:07:11,225 --> 00:07:16,822
The first two phases, from
Bedok to the tip of Tanjong Rhu,

108
00:07:16,902 --> 00:07:21,055
added 458 hectares
between 1966 and 1971 and

109
00:07:21,135 --> 00:07:25,850
created a sandy beach
about nine kilometres long.

110
00:07:25,850 --> 00:07:30,395
The project used artificial headland
breakwaters, one of the first

111
00:07:30,475 --> 00:07:35,100
large-scale uses of the method
anywhere, to shape the new beaches.

112
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:39,457
By 1985 the reclamation had added 1,525

113
00:07:39,537 --> 00:07:43,780
hectares of land along the East Coast,

114
00:07:43,860 --> 00:07:47,500
at a cost of about $613 million.

115
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:52,027
Katong Park, which had been laid
out on the seafront in the 1930s

116
00:07:52,107 --> 00:07:56,422
over the remains of Fort Tanjong
Katong, lost its sea frontage

117
00:07:56,502 --> 00:08:00,463
as soon as the work began,
and the Chinese Swimming Club,

118
00:08:00,543 --> 00:08:05,150
whose pool had once jutted out
into the sea, found itself inland.

119
00:08:05,150 --> 00:08:09,875
Sea Breeze Lodge was left facing
a road instead of a beach.

120
00:08:09,875 --> 00:08:12,893
The new land was used for East Coast Park,

121
00:08:12,973 --> 00:08:15,850
the East Coast Parkway and new housing.

122
00:08:15,850 --> 00:08:19,417
Marine Parade became
the first housing estate

123
00:08:19,497 --> 00:08:23,225
in Singapore built
entirely on reclaimed land.

124
00:08:23,225 --> 00:08:28,237
Its first flats were
put to ballot in 1974,

125
00:08:28,317 --> 00:08:32,500
when a three-room flat cost $13,500,

126
00:08:32,580 --> 00:08:38,975
a four-room flat $20,500 and
a five-room flat $35,500.

127
00:08:38,975 --> 00:08:45,226
By the late 1980s, with the beach,
the park and the shops close by,

128
00:08:45,306 --> 00:08:49,384
it was one of the most
sought-after estates,

129
00:08:49,464 --> 00:08:54,487
and a three-room flat that
had cost $13,500 resold for

130
00:08:54,567 --> 00:08:59,575
about $50,000 in 1988 and
more than $165,000 in 1996.

131
00:08:59,575 --> 00:09:02,688
Meyer Road is now a
street of condominiums,

132
00:09:02,768 --> 00:09:07,075
many of them built on the plots
of the old seaside houses.

133
00:09:07,075 --> 00:09:11,974
The seafront is still part
of their value: Meyer Park,

134
00:09:12,054 --> 00:09:18,151
built in 1984, was described as
offering sea views from every unit,

135
00:09:18,231 --> 00:09:23,025
and in 2023 it was sold en
bloc for $392.18 million.

136
00:09:23,025 --> 00:09:25,553
Crescent Flats and Meyer Flats

137
00:09:25,633 --> 00:09:28,675
were demolished in the early 1990s.

138
00:09:28,675 --> 00:09:32,795
Katong Park MRT station opened on the 23rd

139
00:09:32,875 --> 00:09:37,175
of June 2024, with two
exits on Meyer Road.

140
00:09:37,175 --> 00:09:40,596
Sea Breeze Lodge is the
last bungalow on Marine

141
00:09:40,676 --> 00:09:44,400
Parade Road that was built
before the reclamation.

142
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:48,546
Eric Choa, Choa-Kim-Keat's
grandson, turned down several offers

143
00:09:48,626 --> 00:09:52,450
to redevelop it so that he
could live out his life there.

144
00:09:52,450 --> 00:09:57,324
After his death in 2009, the
47,400-square-foot site was

145
00:09:57,404 --> 00:10:02,898
put up for tender, and in June
2011 it was sold to a subsidiary

146
00:10:02,978 --> 00:10:06,791
of Far East Organization
for $103.8 million,

147
00:10:06,871 --> 00:10:12,975
a price reported at the time as a
new benchmark for land in the area.

148
00:10:12,975 --> 00:10:17,924
The bungalow itself, with a floor
area of about 3,100 square feet,

149
00:10:18,004 --> 00:10:21,353
has been conserved by
the Urban Redevelopment

150
00:10:21,433 --> 00:10:25,700
Authority as part of the Joo
Chiat Secondary Settlement.

151
00:10:25,700 --> 00:10:28,925
The East Coast may yet change again.

152
00:10:28,925 --> 00:10:33,214
The Urban Redevelopment Authority
is studying "Long Island",

153
00:10:33,294 --> 00:10:37,219
a plan to reclaim more than
800 hectares off East Coast

154
00:10:37,299 --> 00:10:41,515
Park to protect the low-lying
coast from rising sea levels,

155
00:10:41,595 --> 00:10:45,600
create a new reservoir and
provide land for future use.

156
00:10:45,600 --> 00:10:49,426
As of its update in March
2026 the design was still

157
00:10:49,506 --> 00:10:52,719
being worked out through
technical studies,

158
00:10:52,799 --> 00:10:57,700
as described in the post on
Singapore's first land reclamations.

159
00:10:57,700 --> 00:11:01,867
Buyers of seafront homes in
the 1950s had no way of knowing

160
00:11:01,947 --> 00:11:05,610
that their beach would be
moved within a generation;

161
00:11:05,690 --> 00:11:10,001
buyers along the East Coast
today can read about the plans in

162
00:11:10,081 --> 00:11:14,400
URA's published updates and
master plans before they decide.

163
00:11:14,400 --> 00:11:18,837
Why it matters today: Tanjong Katong
shows how much of a property's

164
00:11:18,917 --> 00:11:23,300
value can come from its setting,
and how that setting can change.

165
00:11:23,300 --> 00:11:27,301
Dunman's three bungalows, the
Sea View Hotel and the swimming

166
00:11:27,381 --> 00:11:31,247
clubs were all built for the
beach, and the land under them

167
00:11:31,327 --> 00:11:35,475
was divided and sold again and
again because it faced the sea.

168
00:11:35,475 --> 00:11:38,784
The sea has moved several
hundred metres away,

169
00:11:38,864 --> 00:11:42,836
but the old seafront roads
are still lined with private

170
00:11:42,916 --> 00:11:46,888
homes and condominiums, and
one 1898 bungalow on Marine

171
00:11:46,968 --> 00:11:50,725
Parade Road still marks
where the beach used to be.
